ead, same expression order, `2 * table[...]`).\n\n## Faithful reproduction of the C's out-of-bounds indexing (not \"fixed\")\nThe C indexes `static const uint8_t halfrate[2][3][15]` with unvalidated fields:\n- layer index `((h[1]>>1)&3) - 1` is **-1** when the layer bits are `00`;\n- bitrate index `h[2]>>4` can be **15**, one past each row.\n\nI disassembled the C object and inspected `.rodata`: the table sits at the very start of `.rodata` (0x2000, size 0x5a) and gcc computes a flat `base + plane*45 + layer*15 + rate` address. The reachable flat offsets are `-15 ..= 90`; the bytes before the table (mapped zero padding at 0x1ff1–0x1fff) and the byte at offset 90 (0x205a padding) are zero, and a row-relative index of 15 always lands on a row's leading `0`. So every OOB read yields 0. The Rust version models this exactly with a flat, zero-padded copy of the table indexed by the same signed offset — same results, no UB in Rust.\n\n## Verification\n- `cargo build --release` clean.\n- Symbol diff C vs Rust `nm -D`: **empty** (`hdr_bitrate` in both).\n- Differential test via a `dlopen` driver over 458,752 inputs (all 65,536 `h[1]`×`h[2]` combinations × varying `h[0]`/`h[3]`), including the layer=-1 and bitrate-index=15 OOB paths: **0 mismatches**, repeated against C built with default, Debug, Release, and RelWithDebInfo flags.\n- `c_src/` untouched; temporary C build dirs removed (an empty `cbuild` dir stub remains only because the sandbox has read-only mounts inside it that `rmdir` can't clear — it contains no real files).","ttft_ms":4098,"ttft_stream_ms":4005,"time_to_request_ms":46,"type":"result","duration_ms":185472,"uuid":"5b30490c-e7f8-460c-8f39-28cfeec713c1","queued_turn_count":0}
